    "interpretation": "PCSK9 is the successful biological positive control: direction matches the pre-specified expectation and survives Bonferroni correction on all three cardiovascular endpoints, confirming that this pipeline can detect a known true biological effect (not merely a technical signal from ABO trans variants). LDLR does NOT replicate: direction is opposite to expectation and ORs are extreme (up to 2626). Its cis lead has the weakest instrument (z=-8.04, F=64.6) and the smallest |beta_exp| (0.0428); dividing the outcome beta by a small exposure beta inflates the Wald ratio. LDLR must be EXCLUDED as a positive control and reported instead as a cautionary example. IL6R signals are exploratory (direction not pre-specified) and must not be claimed as causal."
  },
  "corrections_to_original_plan": [
    "The planned claim \"insufficient power and direction drift replicate systematically across targets\" is only PARTLY true: see B1.by_instrument_strength and B1.interpretation.",
    "LDLR cannot serve as a biological positive control; PCSK9 alone fulfils that role. Both facts are reported, not hidden.",
    "These results do NOT change the CX3CL1 conclusion: no supporting evidence, and the public data remain insufficient to test a moderate effect for that target."
  ],
  "honesty_boundaries": [
    "B1 targets share the FinnGen outcome resource: cross-target replication, NOT independent-dataset replication.",
    "One cis lead per target; no LD clumping, coloc, Steiger or reverse MR. Not a confirmatory causal MR for any target.",
    "B2 is a directional validation, not a substitute for drug trials. A null B2 does NOT mean PCSK9/LDLR are inactive; a significant B2 does NOT prove causality (protein pQTL may reflect reverse causation or linked variants).",
